 ## Overview
-Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) is a standard benchmark metric used to evaluate the photometric fidelity of reconstructed or rendered images against ground-truth references in differentiable volume rendering pipelines [S1, S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4]. It quantifies the mean squared error (MSE) between predicted and target pixel values, converting this error into a logarithmic decibel (dB) scale where higher values indicate better quality [S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike structural metrics such as SSIM, PSNR focuses strictly on pixel-wise intensity differences, making it sensitive to large smooth regions but less responsive to small, high-frequency features like specular highlights [S1, unofficial].
+Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) is a standard benchmark metric used to evaluate the photometric fidelity of reconstructed or rendered images against ground-truth references in differentiable volume rendering pipelines [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial][S4]. It quantifies the mean squared error (MSE) between predicted and target pixel values, converting this error into a logarithmic decibel (dB) scale where higher values indicate better quality [S2, unofficial]. Unlike structural metrics such as SSIM, PSNR focuses strictly on pixel-wise intensity differences, making it weight large smooth regions far more heavily than small, high-frequency features like specular highlights [S1, unofficial].
 
